# Deduplicating on a value of a type that isn't hashable (Struct here) must be a
# graceful error, not a Rust panic. Value::hash() has a guard
# (`if !self.value_type().is_hashable() { panic!(...) }`) meant to be prevented by
# callers checking is_hashable() first -- sort.rs does this via is_comparable(), but
# uniq.rs had no such check before calling HashSet::contains/insert on a Row, so a
# Struct-typed column reached the panic directly.
#
# A `select`-computed column is always statically declared as $any (a closure's output
# type is never known ahead of time -- see Closure::output_type), so the unhashable
# value only exists at runtime; this can't be caught by checking the column's declared
# type the way sort.rs does, only by checking each actual value as it's processed.
#
# This is a custom Rust assertion (see tests/system.rs) rather than a plain stdout diff:
# a panicking worker thread and a graceful CrushResult::Err both leave this line's stdout
# empty, and whether the panic message itself makes it to stderr before the whole
# process exits is a race in a longer script (confirmed by hand), so this is kept as its
# own short, isolated script instead of being folded into a larger combined test.
#
# uniq must be the *last* command in the pipe, not piped further to echo:
# Job::eval() only tracks/joins the thread for the last command of a pipeline -- a
# non-last stage's spawned thread is never joined at all, so its result (whether a
# panic or, now, a properly-propagated graceful error) never reaches anywhere that
# would report it. That's a separate, deeper gap than what this test is about.
$t := $(csv:from "a,1\nb,2\n" key=$string val=$integer)
$t | select s={struct:of val=$val} | uniq
